</think>

It depends on two things, and I'll give you a rule for each.

**Drop-off asymmetry.** Pull the funnel numbers. If 100% of users who reach step 1 finish all three steps, splitting just inserts inter-step latency for no gain. If 40% bail at step 1 and only 25% reach step 3, splitting step 3 saves 75% of users from downloading code they never run. Real wizards almost always have meaningful drop-off, so this favors splitting — but verify rather than assume.

**Per-step weight.** A wizard where step 1 is a name field, step 2 is an email field, and step 3 is the same shape isn't worth splitting. A wizard where step 3 imports Stripe Elements (~80KB) or a date-range picker with locale data (~60KB) is worth splitting even at high completion rates because the asymmetry is huge.

Here's the pattern that resolves the latency concern: split per step but **prefetch the next step's chunk as soon as the current step mounts**. The user spends 5-30 seconds filling in fields; the next chunk has more than enough time to land.

```tsx
import dynamic from "next/dynamic";
import { useEffect } from "react";

const Step1 = dynamic(() => import("./step-1"));
const Step2 = dynamic(() => import("./step-2"));
const Step3 = dynamic(() => import("./step-3"));

const prefetchStep2 = () => import("./step-2");
const prefetchStep3 = () => import("./step-3");

export function Wizard({ step }: { step: 1 | 2 | 3 }) {
  useEffect(() => {
    const idle = (cb: () => void) =>
      "requestIdleCallback" in window
        ? (window as any).requestIdleCallback(cb, { timeout: 2000 })
        : setTimeout(cb, 200);

    if (step === 1) idle(prefetchStep2);
    if (step === 2) idle(prefetchStep3);
  }, [step]);

  if (step === 1) return <Step1 />;
  if (step === 2) return <Step2 />;
  return <Step3 />;
}
```

`requestIdleCallback` is the right primitive here — unlike the chart computation we discussed, this is genuinely background work the user isn't waiting for. The 2000ms timeout ensures the prefetch happens even on a busy page. The dynamic import is idempotent; if the chunk's already in the browser cache from your prefetch, the actual `<Step2 />` render uses it instantly.

**The over-splitting trap, concretely.** Don't split individual form fields, individual buttons, or anything under ~5KB gzipped. Don't split components used in the same render frame as their parent (like a header that always renders with its layout). The bookkeeping cost — chunk manifest entries, HTTP requests, parse overhead — exceeds the benefit.

**Diagnose if you've over-split.** Open DevTools Network panel, filter to JS, sort by size, and look at the long tail. If you see 20+ chunks under 3KB each, you've over-split — bundle them. The `splitChunks.minSize` config in webpack/Next defaults to 20KB precisely to prevent this; if you've lowered it, you've probably gone too far.

For your wizard: if step 3 has the heavy dependencies, split it and prefetch on step 2 mount. If steps are weight-symmetric, keep them in one chunk and split only the section itself. Numbers over instinct — `next build --analyze` after each change tells you whether you actually moved the needle.", "role": "assistant"}], "model": "claude-opus-4-7"}, "record_metadata": {"category": "coding", "model": "claude-opus-4-7"}, "source_label": "train", "source_path": "data/hf_datasets/angrygiraffe__claude-opus-4.6-4.7-reasoning-8.7k__coding"}, "role": "user", "row_index": 361, "source_dataset": "angrygiraffe__claude-opus-4.6-4.7-reasoning-8.7k__coding", "source_dataset_group": "angrygiraffe__claude-opus-4.6-4.7-reasoning-8.7k__coding", "source_language": null, "source_text": "Quick clarification on granularity. You said split at section boundaries, but what about within a section — say the signup section has a multi-step wizard with three steps. Should each step be its own chunk, or is that the over-splitting trap?", "source_type": "question", "split": "train", "target_language": "cs", "turn_index": 3}

